+# Feature Specification: JSON Merge Patch
+
+**Standard**: RFC 7396 (JSON Merge Patch), section 2 (the MergePatch algorithm) and section 3 (examples).
+The request named RFC 7386; RFC 7396 obsoletes it with the same algorithm (only the Appendix A example
+was corrected), and the constitution names 7396. Values are JSON per RFC 8259.
+
+## User Scenarios & Testing
+### Story 1 - Apply a partial update (P1)
+`applyMergePatch(target, patch)` returns the merged document as a new value; inputs are never mutated.
+- Object patch: members merged recursively; a member whose patch value is `null` is removed from the target
+  (absent members are ignored); other members are added or replaced.
+- Non-object patch (array, string, number, boolean, `null`) replaces the target entirely (arrays are not merged).
+- Object patch applied to a non-object target (including array or `null`) merges into `{}`.
+### Story 2 - Reject non-JSON input (P1)
+Throws `JsonMergePatchError` when target or patch is not a JSON value (`undefined`, `NaN`, `Date`, `Map`, ...).
+
+## Requirements
+- FR-1 Follows the section 2 pseudocode exactly, including: `null` inside a patch object that is merged into a
+  non-object target is dropped; a patch of `null` yields `null`.
+- FR-2 Inputs not mutated; the result shares no structure with either input.
+- FR-3 `__proto__`, `constructor`, `prototype` keys are plain data keys.
+- FR-4 Member order of the target is kept; new members are appended.
+
+## Assumptions
+- Public API addition (minor). README updated. A merge patch cannot set a value to `null` (RFC 7396 section 1).
